!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
Relative indexes
description: the dot in the brackets — the host frame, aligned by depth
- |
  A dot in the brackets makes the index **relative to the pointer's own position**:
  `[.]` is "my position at this depth", `[.-1]` one before me, `[.+2]` two after.
  Bracket bodies stay disjoint by form — digits absolute, dot relative, `?` the query
  wildcard — and a plain `[-1]` is deliberately not taken, kept free for a possible
  future from-the-end index.
- |
  Resolution is the **frame rule**: the frame is the host entry's own path — the
  entry holding the pointer. After the base and any `..` ascents, each step consumes
  one depth, and a `[.±k]` at depth d selects the host's position at that depth,
  offset by k. Keyed entries hold positions too, so the frame always exists.
- !!yo
  - *[.-1]        # the cell to my LEFT — a colspan
  - *..[.-1][.]   # the cell ABOVE: up to the table, previous
                  #   row, my column — a rowspan
- |
  The motivating idiom is the table — a merged cell names its neighbor. A relative
  index yields at most one successor, so it is a **link**, authorable after `*`; out
  of range is the ordinary dangling diagnostic. In anchors it is **rejected** — a
  position claim is outlawed, and a relative claim is still a claim — and in queries
  too: a query has no host frame.
